Lab 19 - Network Troubleshooting in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Inspect OpenStack network namespaces and port bindings
Capture and analyze network traffic using tcpdump/Wireshark
Troubleshoot common networking issues including:
Security group misconfigurations
IP address conflicts
Neutron agent failures
Prerequisites
OpenStack cloud deployment (DevStack or production)
SSH access to controller and compute nodes
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient)
Basic Linux networking knowledge
tcpdump and iproute2 packages installed
Wireshark (optional for GUI analysis)
Task 1: Inspect Network Namespaces and Port Bindings
1.1 List All Network Namespaces
sudo ip netns list
Expected Output:
Lists all network namespaces (qrouter-, qdhcp-, etc.)

Troubleshooting Tip:
If no namespaces appear, ensure neutron services are running:

sudo systemctl status neutron-*
1.2 Inspect Specific Namespace
sudo ip netns exec qrouter-<UUID> ip a
Replace <UUID> with actual router ID from openstack router list

Key Concept:
Each router/DHCP service runs in its own network namespace

1.3 Check Port Bindings
openstack port list --network <NETWORK_ID>
openstack port show <PORT_ID>
Expected Outcome:
Verify binding:vif_type and status fields are correct values

Task 2: Capture Network Traffic
2.1 Identify Tap Interfaces
ip link show | grep tap
brctl show
2.2 Capture Traffic with tcpdump
sudo tcpdump -i tap<ID> -w /tmp/openstack_traffic.pcap
Pro Tip:
Filter specific traffic with:

sudo tcpdump -i tap<ID> 'port 67 or port 68' -w dhcp.pcap  # DHCP traffic
2.3 Analyze in Wireshark (Optional)
wireshark /tmp/openstack_traffic.pcap
Key Analysis Points:

ARP requests/responses
DHCP handshakes
ICMP unreachable messages
Task 3: Troubleshoot Common Issues
3.1 Security Group Misconfigurations
openstack security group list
openstack security group rule list <SG_ID>
Verification Test:

openstack server add security group <INSTANCE> <SG>
3.2 Detect IP Conflicts
sudo ip netns exec qdhcp-<UUID> arping -c 3 <IP_ADDRESS>
Expected Outcome:
Single response = valid, multiple responses = conflict

3.3 Check Neutron Agents
openstack network agent list
Healthy State:
Alive = True, State = UP

Restart Failed Agents:

sudo systemctl restart neutron-l3-agent
Conclusion
In this lab you have:

Examined OpenStack network namespaces and port bindings
Captured and analyzed network traffic at the virtualization layer
Diagnosed three common network issues in OpenStack environments
Final Verification:

openstack network test create --name test-net
openstack server create --image cirros --flavor m1.tiny --network test-net test-vm
